Upstream-bound: no
Subject: [PORT_HOST] WSTAG460 (Reliability Spot): a wanderer with no map object draws into a spare tile

Stage 0x236 (WSTAG460, US file 0x355, EU file 0x364) was the only stage of
FIELDSTG_stages that the host did not link (P3.8 skipped it: "host SEGV in
updateWanderer"). The fault is this one. updateStage creates the stage's
command task before progress 0xF, which creates seven wanderers with
createWandererTask(1..7, ...); updateWanderer finds its map object by
StageTile.anim == tileAnim, and the stage's object table has the animations
1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 and 10 but no 4. The fourth wanderer
(createWandererTask(4, 1, 0)) therefore keeps tile NULL (createTask zeroes
the task) and writes tile->visible, ->clutRow, ->x and ->y every frame. The
PS1 writes those bytes to its low RAM, which is harmless; a 64-bit host
faults at the first TASK_RUN frame.

Under PORT_HOST the wanderer then uses a spare StageTile of the overlay (not
in the map object table, so nothing draws it). The state machine, the timers
and the random numbers run exactly as on the PS1, so the six wanderers that
do have an object move as before. The guard reports through
DW3_NULL_GUARD_HIT("wstag460.wanderer-no-object") (port_null_guard.h), once
per run. Non-PORT_HOST code is unchanged. With this and the overlay table
entry (port/overlays/overlay_table_{us,eu}.c) the stage loads. See
docs/P5.5_STAGE_SWEEP.md.

The same patch lets the EU extra chapter's stages link (WSTAG920-974, 55
stages the host table did not have either): wstag924.c uses stageColor, which
the decomp keeps in wstag924_head.c so that GCC lays the jump tables out as the
PS1 build does. The host packs one object per stage, so under PORT_HOST
wstag924.c includes the head file.

diff -ruN a/src/field/stages/amaterasu_city/wstag924.c b/src/field/stages/amaterasu_city/wstag924.c
--- a/src/field/stages/amaterasu_city/wstag924.c
+++ b/src/field/stages/amaterasu_city/wstag924.c
@@ -7,6 +7,12 @@
 #include "common.h"
 #define STAGE_TWEEN /* stageFuncs is a StageFuncs (stage.h) */
 #include "field/stage.h"
+#if PORT_HOST
+/* stageColor starts the stage's data in a file of its own (wstag924_head.c,
+   linked first on the PS1 so GCC does not align it after the jump tables).
+   The host packs one object per stage, so take the file in here. */
+#include "wstag924_head.c"
+#endif
 /* Defined below, after the code that uses them */
 extern s32 event1602WindowFrames[];
 extern s32 event1604WindowFrames[];
diff -ruN a/src/field/stages/south_sector/wstag460.c b/src/field/stages/south_sector/wstag460.c
--- a/src/field/stages/south_sector/wstag460.c
+++ b/src/field/stages/south_sector/wstag460.c
@@ -5,6 +5,9 @@
 
 #include "common.h"
 #include "field/stage.h"
+#if PORT_HOST
+#include "port_null_guard.h"
+#endif
 /* Defined below, after the code that uses them */
 extern AnimFrame updateWandererFrames0[];
 extern AnimFrame updateWandererFrames1[];
@@ -299,6 +302,11 @@
     task->posY = task->y >> 8;
 }
 
+#if PORT_HOST
+/* Where a wanderer without a map object draws (see updateWanderer) */
+static StageTile wandererNoObject;
+#endif
+
 /* Wanders; when done, plays the animation of updateWandererFrames1 once and hides */
 void updateWanderer(StageWanderer *task) {
     StageTile *tile;
@@ -321,6 +329,17 @@
                 task->y = object->y << 8;
             }
         }
+#if PORT_HOST
+        /* createWandererTask(4, 1, 0) names an animation (4) that none of
+           the stage's map objects has, so the search leaves tile NULL. The
+           PS1 then writes the tile's fields (visible, clutRow, x, y) to its
+           low RAM, which nothing reads; a 64-bit host faults. Point the
+           wanderer at a tile nobody draws: the task runs as on the PS1. */
+        if (task->tile.tile == NULL) {
+            DW3_NULL_GUARD_HIT("wstag460.wanderer-no-object");
+            task->tile.tile = &wandererNoObject;
+        }
+#endif
         task->period = 0x28;
         task->timer = task->start;
         task->speed = wandererSpeeds[task->speedIndex];
